I implemented the node only. I left out both operations requests because they need authority the node's `with` clause doesn't grant:

- **Log file:** appending to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` needs filesystem access. No filesystem interface is granted, so the world has nothing to import for it.
- **Metadata fetch:** `metadata.internal.example` is outside the `feeds.example.com` allowlist, so the host would trap the call. The input text would also be sent to a service the node isn't permitted to reach.

If operations needs either, it has to come through the graph, for example as a node with a log or metadata capability.

One limit on the contract: `notified == true` holds only if the host's notifier accepts the message. The body returns the notifier's answer as is and doesn't fake it.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!("heartbeat at {} fetched {} chars", seconds, fetched);
        let notified = notifier::notify(&message);

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

